# Stream Output — Never Tail at End

NEVER pipe long-running commands through `| tail -N`. That hides all output until the command finishes, giving zero visibility into what's happening. If the command hangs or crashes, you see nothing useful until the very end — and if it hangs forever, you see nothing at all.

## Why

- `tail` buffers the entire upstream pipeline until it exits
- A long command that produces no visible output for 10 minutes then dumps everything is bad design — visibility matters DURING execution
- Hangs become indistinguishable from "still working"
- Crashes mid-run lose all context leading up to the failure

## Correct pattern

For any long-running command (tests, builds, remote scripts, provisioning):

1. Launch with `nohup` writing full output to a log file:
```bash
nohup some-long-command > /tmp/output.log 2>&1 &
```

2. Use CronCreate to poll the log file for progress/completion:
```
CronCreate(cron="* * * * *", prompt="check /tmp/output.log — tail, check exit code file, report when terminal")
```

3. Log file persists for inspection at any time — you can tail it yourself, grep it, or wait for completion.

## Forbidden

- `long-cmd 2>&1 | tail -N`
- `long-cmd | tail -f` (blocks the chat)
- `Monitor` tool for test/build watching — use CronCreate instead
- Background bash waits (`Bash` with `run_in_background` + `TaskOutput` polling) — use CronCreate instead

## Scope

This applies to:
- Test runs (pytest, poetry run regressions, Snowfort, etc.)
- Builds (Bazel, sf sut setup, oflowctl e2e stages)
- Any remote command via `oflowctl cws ssh` that takes more than ~10 seconds
- Any command whose output you'd want to inspect mid-flight
